{
  "stages": {
    "prototype": {
      "level": 4,
      "rationale": "The only goal is to learn whether an idea is worth pursuing, so speed of exploration dominates and the code is expected to be discarded. Accepting unread AI output is fine as long as you personally try the behavior and don't let the prototype quietly become the foundation of something real."
    },
    "proof-of-concept": {
      "level": 3,
      "rationale": "A demo only needs to work convincingly in the paths stakeholders will see, so selective review plus behavior checks is usually enough. But demos have a habit of becoming the seed of the real product, so keep the code isolated and flag anything you would not want to inherit."
    },
    "mvp": {
      "level": 3,
      "rationale": "Real users generate real data, real edge cases, and real trust, so you should be reading the code that touches auth, payments, data handling, and anything hard to reverse. Elsewhere, selective review backed by tests is a reasonable trade for the speed early validation requires."
    },
    "production": {
      "level": 2,
      "rationale": "With paying customers and a maintaining team, unread code becomes an ongoing liability for debugging, security, and onboarding, and the cost of a subtle bug exceeds the time saved. Review every change line by line, treating AI output as a draft from a fast but unreliable contributor."
    },
    "critical": {
      "level": 1,
      "rationale": "In regulated or safety-critical systems, defects can injure people or violate law, and accountability must attach to a named human who understands every line. Let AI suggest and draft, but require a person to write or fully rewrite the final code, backed by formal review, traceability, and independent verification."
    }
  },
  "summary": "Vibe coding is a tool for buying speed where mistakes are cheap and reversible, not a default posture. A good rule of thumb: the closer code gets to real users, money, or harm, the more of it a human must actually read. Loosen review for throwaway exploration (levels 3-4) and tighten it steadily as the blast radius of failure grows, ending at level 1 for critical systems where human authorship and accountability are non-negotiable."
}

